const t={title:"Massive data + next-word guessing",mc:"var(--io)",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">The training exercises: cut a passage from text and guess the word that follows.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Massive data + next-word guessing</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labNext" data-lv="T3">🧪 Word-chain master (LLM)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>Huge amounts of text — articles, books, code — are cut into passages, and every position becomes a question: “Given all this, what’s the next word?” The source text carries its own answer key.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Data is collected, cleaned and de-duplicated by people; it isn’t a model parameter.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>The data itself isn’t adjusted; all of the model’s parameters are adjusted around “guessing these questions better”.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Training data is no longer needed in use; the model only uses its learned parameters.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Text carries its own answers: every word is the “correct answer” for the text before it, so no human labelling is needed.</li><li>That means almost any text can become practice questions, and the amount of data can be staggering.</li></ul></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><ul class="pts"><li>To guess well on all kinds of text, memorizing surface word pairings isn’t enough: guessing “The capital of France is __” takes knowledge, guessing the conclusion of an argument takes logic, and guessing the next sentence takes grammar and context.</li><li>To push the loss down, the model is forced to “compress” these regularities — grammar, facts, logic — into its parameters.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: What the model learns is “the regularities common in text”, including the errors and biases in it; how much reasoning “guessing the next word well” can teach is still debated.</p></div>'},e={title:"Loss (cross-entropy)",mc:"var(--out)",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">A single number: how badly this question was guessed.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Loss (cross-entropy)</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labGraph" data-lv="N">🧪 Backprop step by step (Neural Network Basics)</button><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labFit" data-lv="T7">🧪 Learning rate &amp; overfitting (Training &amp; RL)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>Loss = −log(probability given to the correct word). Give the correct word 70% → about 0.36; only 10% → about 2.3; 100% → 0.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>A fixed formula with no parameters.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>It is training’s “baton”: every parameter adjustment aims to make it smaller.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>The loss isn’t computed in use.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Billions of parameters need one shared goal, ideally “a single number”: the smaller the better, so that “which way to adjust” makes sense.</li><li>Use the negative log of the correct word’s probability: as the probability approaches 1 the loss approaches 0</li><li>as it approaches 0 the loss grows</li><li>being wrong and very confident is punished hardest.</li></ul></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Paired with softmax, the gradient is especially clean: the adjustment to each word’s score = predicted probability − correct answer (1 or 0).</li><li>This signal neither explodes nor vanishes, and its size reflects exactly how wrong it was.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: Cross-entropy is the most common choice and makes sense from the viewpoint of probability and information theory, but it isn’t the only possible loss.</p></div>'},a={title:"Backpropagation + gradient descent",mc:"var(--blk)",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Works out which way each parameter should move, then takes a small step.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Backpropagation + gradient descent</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labGraph" data-lv="N">🧪 Backprop step by step (Neural Network Basics)</button><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labFit" data-lv="T7">🧪 Learning rate &amp; overfitting (Training &amp; RL)</button><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labBlock" data-lv="T4">🧪 Build a Transformer block (Transformer block)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Backpropagation: starting from the loss, the chain rule works backwards layer by layer to compute “if this parameter changes a little, how much does the loss change” (the gradient).</li><li>Gradient descent: every parameter takes a small step in the direction that makes the loss smaller.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>A fixed algorithm; the step size (learning rate) and so on are set by people.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>This is “training” itself: every step adjusts all learnable parameters.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>No backpropagation in use; the parameters are frozen.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><ul class="pts"><li>With billions or even hundreds of billions of parameters, hand-tuning is impossible.</li><li>The chain rule efficiently computes each parameter’s “responsibility” for the error (how big an effect and in which direction), at a total cost of only a few times the forward computation.</li></ul></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Like walking downhill blindfolded: each time, take a small step in the steepest downhill direction underfoot; repeat many, many times and the loss keeps going down.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: It doesn’t guarantee finding the “global minimum”, only heading downhill nearby; but for large networks it works very well in practice, and why is still being researched.</p></div>'},i={title:"Artificial neural network",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">A mathematical function with lots of “knobs”: numbers in, numbers out, with the knobs tuned automatically from data.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Artificial neural network</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 Has learnable parameters: adjusted in training, read-only in use</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labTiny" data-lv="N">🧪 Train a tiny network (Neural Network Basics)</button><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labFit" data-lv="T7">🧪 Learning rate &amp; overfitting (Training &amp; RL)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Many neurons arranged in layers, connected layer to layer.</li><li>The forward pass gives a prediction, the loss measures how wrong it is, backpropagation works out which way to turn each knob, and gradient descent takes a small step.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Parameters are randomly initialized.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Training is the adjusting of these parameters.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Parameters are frozen; only the forward pass runs.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>No hand-written rules — give it examples and let it find the patterns itself; the structure is general and works on almost any data.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>With non-linear activations, stacked layers can approximate very complex functions; gradients make “which way to turn” computable.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: It learns statistical correlations, not causal understanding; it’s hard to interpret; “neuron” is just a biologically inspired name.</p></div>'},s={title:"Artificial neuron",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Weighted sum + activation: the smallest unit of computation.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Artificial neuron</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 Has learnable parameters: adjusted in training, read-only in use</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labNeuron" data-lv="N">🧪 One neuron (Neural Network Basics)</button><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labFit" data-lv="T7">🧪 Learning rate &amp; overfitting (Training &amp; RL)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>z = w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + … + b, then a = f(z), where f can be ReLU (negatives become 0) and the like.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Weights and bias are randomly initialized.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Weights and bias are adjusted.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Computes by the formula.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The weighted sum expresses “how important each input is”; the activation introduces a “bend”.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Many “kinked lines” put together can approximate curvy shapes.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: A single neuron can only draw a straight-line boundary.</p></div>'},l={title:"Architectures: MLP / CNN / RNN / Transformer",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">How neurons are connected: different wiring suits different data.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Architectures: MLP / CNN / RNN / Transformer</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labBlock" data-lv="T4">🧪 Build a Transformer block (Transformer block)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>MLP: fully connected</li><li>CNN: a small window slides over the image with shared weights</li><li>RNN/LSTM: reads in order and maintains a memory</li><li>Transformer: every position looks directly at every position.</li><li>Today’s LLMs mainly use the Transformer.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Designed by people.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>The structure isn’t trained; the parameters inside it are.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Determines how computation happens.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>An architecture hides “prior assumptions” about the data; when they fit the data, it needs less data and generalizes more easily.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>The Transformer assumes the least, performs generally well when data is plentiful, and suits GPU parallelism extremely well.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: There is no absolute best, and the boundaries are blurring (hybrid architectures, new sequence models and so on are being explored).</p></div>'},n={title:"Self-supervised learning",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Makes exercises from the data itself, without human labelling.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Self-supervised learning</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labNext" data-lv="T3">🧪 Word-chain master (LLM)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>“To be or not to __” → guess “be”; cut a piece out of an image and have the model fill it in; two crops of the same image should be similar. The answer is hidden in the data.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>It is a way of training.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Determines what exercises the parameters are trained on.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Not involved in use.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Human labelling is expensive and slow; self-supervision can use an almost unlimited amount of data.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>To get the exercises right, the model is forced to learn grammar, common sense and visual regularities.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: It learns “what is common in the data”, including errors and biases.</p></div>'},d={title:"Pretraining",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">“Guess the next word” on huge amounts of text (and code, etc.), laying the foundation of language and knowledge.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Pretraining</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 This is a training stage: model parameters are updated here</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labNext" data-lv="T3">🧪 Word-chain master (LLM)</button><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labFit" data-lv="T7">🧪 Learning rate &amp; overfitting (Training &amp; RL)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>The data is cut into passages, and every position becomes a “what’s the next word” question</li><li>training processes an enormous number of tokens and runs for a long time on large GPU clusters.</li><li>The result is called a “base model”: it can continue text but doesn’t necessarily follow instructions.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Starts from random parameters; the data is collected, cleaned, de-duplicated and mixed in proportions.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>All parameters are adjusted; this is the most compute-hungry stage.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Base models are rarely given to users directly; they serve as the starting point for further training.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Text carries its own answers, so data is almost unlimited; “guessing the next word well” takes knowledge and reasoning, making it a goal that draws out general abilities.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Empirically, the bigger the model, the more data and the longer the training, the lower the loss and the stronger the abilities (scaling laws), but returns diminish.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: It needs enormous compute; data quality and mix are critical; learned biases are hard to remove completely.</p></div>'},o={title:"Supervised fine-tuning (instruction tuning)",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Uses human-written “question → high-quality answer” demonstrations to teach the model to follow instructions and answer in dialogue format.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Supervised fine-tuning (instruction tuning)</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 This is a training stage: model parameters are updated here</span></div><div class="tryit"><span class="hint">🧪 There’s no dedicated simulation for this part yet; browse “All labs” on the “Reference” stop.</span></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>The data looks like this: {"user":"Explain photosynthesis in three sentences","assistant":"…"}</li><li>it also includes tool-call demonstrations (when to output which JSON).</li><li>Training is still “guess the next word”, except the loss is computed only on the “assistant answer” part.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Starts from the pretrained base model.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Parameters are adjusted (all of them, or only a small part with LoRA and the like).</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>The formats and habits learned show up in use.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>A base model “knows a lot” but doesn’t get “you’re asking me, so I should answer”. Demonstration data teaches format and habits, not knowledge from scratch.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><ul class="pts"><li>The abilities are already there from pretraining, so a small number of high-quality demonstrations is enough to “activate” and “steer” them</li><li>data quality matters more than quantity (empirically).</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: It can only imitate the demonstrations, mistakes included; it’s hard to teach it to be “better than the demonstrations”.</p></div>'},r={title:"Preference alignment (RLHF etc.)",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Has people compare “which answer is better” and uses those preferences to adjust the model.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Preference alignment (RLHF etc.)</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 This is a training stage: model parameters are updated here</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn" data-jump="rlhfCard" data-lv="T7">▶ You be the labeller</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>① Generate several answers to the same question</li><li>② people compare which is better</li><li>③ train a reward model to imitate human preferences in its scores</li><li>④ use reinforcement learning to make the model’s answers score higher with the reward model, with a KL constraint to keep it from straying too far from the original model.</li><li>Some methods skip the reward model and optimize directly on preference pairs (e.g. DPO).</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Starts from the fine-tuned model.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Parameters are adjusted.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Makes answers more useful, more honest, safer and more agreeable.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>A “good answer” is hard to write down as rules or demonstrations, but people find it easy to compare which of two answers is better.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Comparison data is cheap and consistent; the reward model compresses that feeling into a function that can score automatically, enabling optimization at scale.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: The model may learn to flatter, ramble, or sound confident without being right (reward hacking); human preferences themselves are subjective and biased.</p></div>'},b={title:"Reasoning RL (verifiable rewards)",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">For problems like maths and code where right and wrong can be checked automatically, let the model try several times and reward it when it’s right.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Reasoning RL (verifiable rewards)</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 This is a training stage: model parameters are updated here</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn" data-jump="rlhfCard" data-lv="T7">▶ You be the labeller</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Have the model write several solutions to the same problem (including the reasoning), let a program check whether the answer is right or the code passes the tests, and use policy-gradient-style methods to raise the probability of “the processes that led to correct answers”.</li><li>Some methods (e.g. GRPO) compare the attempts at the same problem against each other, saving a separate value model.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Starts from the aligned model.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Parameters are adjusted.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Empirically makes the model better at step-by-step reasoning, self-checking and multi-step tasks.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The reward is objective (right is right), so it isn’t easily fooled by “flattery” and can be trained at scale with confidence.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Through lots of trial and error the model discovers for itself which ways of thinking are more likely to succeed, rather than just imitating steps written by people.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: Mainly applies to domains with clear right and wrong; the model may “guess the right answer with wrong reasoning” or game the test cases; the written-out reasoning doesn’t necessarily reflect the internal computation faithfully.</p></div>'},p={title:"Reinforcement learning",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Learns to make decisions through “trial and error + reward”, aiming to maximize the long-term cumulative reward.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Reinforcement learning</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labGw" data-lv="T7">🧪 RL maze (Training &amp; RL)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Five roles: agent, environment, state, action, reward.</li><li>The loop: observe the state → choose an action → receive a reward and a new state → update the policy.</li><li>Two main families of methods: estimate “value” (e.g. Q-learning), or adjust the “policy” directly (policy gradients).</li><li>LLM training mostly uses policy-gradient methods: PPO (lets the policy change only in small steps and uses a separate “value network” to estimate a baseline score), and group-comparison methods such as GRPO: sample several answers to the same question, subtract the group’s average score from each answer’s score, strengthen the above-average ones and weaken the below-average ones, with no separate value network.</li><li>Why: for an LLM the value network is nearly as large as the model itself and is costly and unstable to train</li><li>using the group average as the baseline saves memory and is simpler, and suits questions that can be checked automatically especially well.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>The policy/value usually starts random.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>The policy or value estimates are adjusted according to rewards.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>The learned policy is used directly.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>For many problems you can’t say “what to do at each step”, but you can judge “whether the outcome was good”.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>The signal of a good outcome flows back along the path taken, gradually teaching which actions lead to good outcomes.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: Low sample efficiency, needing lots of trial and error; poorly designed rewards get exploited.</p></div>'},c={title:"Q-learning",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Estimates a long-term score Q for every “state + action” and corrects it after each step.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Q-learning</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labGw" data-lv="T7">🧪 RL maze (Training &amp; RL)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>New Q = old Q + learning rate × (this step’s reward + γ × best Q of the next state − old Q). The good news from the goal “flows back” one cell at a time.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>The Q table starts at all 0.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Updated at every step.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Takes the action with the highest Q value.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Splitting “good in the long run?” into “immediate reward + estimate for the next step” lets it learn as it goes.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>In simple, finite problems, sufficient exploration converges to the optimum (with theoretical guarantees).</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: With many states the table no longer fits, so a neural network is used to approximate it (deep reinforcement learning).</p></div>'},m={title:"Reward model",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Learns to imitate human preferences and scores answers.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Reward model</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 Has learnable parameters: adjusted in training, read-only in use</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn" data-jump="rlhfCard" data-lv="T7">▶ You be the labeller</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>Input: “question + answer”; output: a score. The training data is human comparisons, “A is better than B”, and the goal is for A to score higher than B.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Usually adapted from a language model.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Trained on preference comparison data.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Scores the model’s answers automatically during the RL stage.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>People can’t score millions of answers in real time, so a “stand-in judge” is needed.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Generalizes a limited number of human comparisons to answers it has never seen.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: When the reward model has loopholes, the policy will exploit them (reward hacking).</p></div>'},h={title:"KL constraint",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">A brake that says “don’t stray too far from the original model”.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>KL constraint</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn" data-jump="rlhfCard" data-lv="T7">▶ You be the labeller</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>Adds a penalty on top of the reward: the more the new model’s output distribution differs from the original model’s, the bigger the penalty.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>A fixed formula, with a coefficient set by people.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Only has an effect during training.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Not computed in use.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Chasing reward alone makes the model weird (saying strange things to farm points).</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Keeps the original language ability and knowledge, fine-tuning only in the direction of “more agreeable”.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: Too large a coefficient and it can’t learn; too small and it easily drifts.</p></div>'},u={title:"DPO (direct preference optimization)",mc:"#65a30d",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">Adjusts the model directly with “A is better than B” data, without training a separate reward model.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>DPO (direct preference optimization)</b></div><span class="status yes">🔥 Has learnable parameters: adjusted in training, read-only in use</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn" data-jump="rlhfCard" data-lv="T7">▶ You be the labeller</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><p>Mathematically rewrites the objective of “reward model + reinforcement learning + KL constraint” as a loss applied directly to preference pairs: raise the probability of good answers relative to the original model, lower that of bad ones.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Starts from the fine-tuned model.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Parameters are adjusted.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>Results similar to RLHF.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>The process is simpler and more stable, needing neither online sampling nor a separate reward model.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>Turns a reinforcement learning problem into something like supervised learning, which is much easier to engineer.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: It relies on fixed preference data and can’t improve while exploring the way online RL can; which method is better depends on the scenario.</p></div>'},v={title:"Data and compute",mc:"#475569",body:'<div class="m-meta"><p class="role">The two fuels of training: huge amounts of high-quality data + large GPU clusters.</p><div class="where">📍 Location: <a data-go="T1">App overview</a> › <a data-go="T7">Training &amp; RL</a> › <b>Data and compute</b></div><span class="status no">⚙️ No learnable parameters: a fixed program / fixed rule / value computed on the fly</span></div><div class="tryit"><b>🧪 Try it: </b><button class="btn primary" data-jump="labFit" data-lv="T7">🧪 Learning rate &amp; overfitting (Training &amp; RL)</button></div><div class="m-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Card sections"><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t0" aria-controls="mt@p0" aria-selected="true" tabindex="0">🎯 Role</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t1" aria-controls="mt@p1" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🌱 Origin</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t2" aria-controls="mt@p2" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🔧 Training</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t3" aria-controls="mt@p3" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">▶ In use</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t4" aria-controls="mt@p4" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">🧠 Why</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="mt@t5" aria-controls="mt@p5" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">⚠️ Limits</button></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p0" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t0" tabindex="0"><div class="sec"><h4>🎯 Role</h4><ul class="pts"><li>Data has to be collected, cleaned, de-duplicated, filtered for harmful content and balanced across domains</li><li>compute means thousands of accelerators working together, with the model and data split across them in parallel, running for a long time.</li></ul></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p1" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t1" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec"><h4>🌱 Where it starts</h4><p>Invested and organized by people.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t2" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#fff7ed"><h4>🔧 How training adjusts it</h4><p>Not parameters, but they determine how big a model can be trained and for how long.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t3" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec" style="background:#f0f9ff"><h4>▶ What it does in use</h4><p>In use, inference compute is needed instead.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t4" tabindex="0" hidden=""><div class="sec why"><h4>🧠 Why it’s designed this way (the logic underneath)</h4><p>Empirically, performance improves steadily with data, parameters and compute (scaling laws), which have to be balanced sensibly.</p></div><div class="sec eff"><h4>✅ Why this works</h4><p>High-quality data makes every unit of compute worth more; parallelism gets training done in an acceptable time.</p></div></div><div class="m-panel" id="mt@p5" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="mt@t5" tabindex="0" hidden=""><p class="lim">⚠️ Limits / notes: Cost and energy use are enormous; high-quality data will eventually run short; scaling laws are empirical observations and won’t hold forever.</p></div>'},y={data:t,loss:e,backprop:a,nnet:i,neuron:s,arch:l,ssl:n,pretrain:d,sft:o,pref:r,rlvr:b,rl:p,qlearn:c,rm:m,kl:h,dpo:u,compute:v};export{l as arch,a as backprop,v as compute,t as data,y as default,u as dpo,h as kl,e as loss,s as neuron,i as nnet,r as pref,d as pretrain,c as qlearn,p as rl,b as rlvr,m as rm,o as sft,n as ssl};
